// error: `T` does not conform to `Sendable`, the bound of `T` in `spawn`: it is a type parameter that may stand for any type; bound it, as in `[T: Sendable]`
// error: what is passed for `body` of `spawn` may use only sendable values from around it, but it uses `x`, a `T`, which is a type parameter that may stand for any type; bound it, as in `[T: Sendable]`
// error: what is passed for `body` of `Future.apply` may use only sendable values from around it, but it uses `b`, a `Buffer[int]`, which is mutable; an immutable copy (`toVector`) is sendable
// error: what is passed for `body` of `Future.apply` may use only sendable values from around it, but it captures `total`, a `var`, which the closure would share with the code that made it; copy it into a `val`, or share it through an `Atomic` or a `Mutex`
// error: what is passed for `body` of `spawn` may use only sendable values from around it, but it uses `s`, a `Shape`, which is a trait that does not promise to be sendable; declare it `trait Shape: Sendable`, and every type implementing it is checked to be
// A by-name body (`Future(…)`) is checked as a lambda is: a mutable buffer and a captured `var` are
// rejected. A generic function passing an unbounded `T` into a thread is rejected twice, for the value
// captured and for the result, and a value of a trait that makes no promise is not sendable.
import avin.threads.{Future, spawn}

trait Shape
    area() -> real

run[T](x: T) -> T = spawn(() -> x).join()

main()
    val b = Buffer[int]()
    println(Future(b.length).await())
    var total = 0
    println(Future(total).await())
    val s: Shape = Sq()
    println(spawn(() -> s.area()).join())

class Sq(): Shape
    override area() -> real = 1.0
